{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01STFTsF9dXhQJgsnm4R5GwU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft reference gap analysis\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready. The draft is well supported overall: the methods (EM, stacking, NNLS, submodular selection, ridge), item-level cheaper-evaluation work, and latent-capability work are all cited, and the quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. Three targeted gaps remain. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n4\t\n5\t## Targets\n6\t\n7\t1. **related-work-comparators** (Related Work, high, comparator)\n8\t   - Anchor: \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices\"; \"here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\".\n9\t   - The closest prior work predicts unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, applies low-rank or matrix-completion methods to LLM score matrices, or selects what to run adaptively. The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources and item-level work. The paper's differentiation claim is therefore not positioned against its direct competitors.\n10\t   - Search topics: predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a benchmark subset; matrix completion or low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark matrices; adaptive or IRT-based active selection for LLM evaluation.\n11\t\n12\t2. **intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim** (Introduction, medium, positioning)\n13\t   - Anchor: \"are known to overlap in what they measure\".\n14\t   - The premise that motivates budgeted evaluation is cited only to the suites themselves, which do not document redundancy.\n15\t   - Search topics: correlation or redundancy among LLM benchmarks; low-dimensional structure in benchmark scores.\n16\t\n17\t3. **data-open-llm-leaderboard-context** (Method, protocol data paragraph, medium, benchmark_context)\n18\t   - Anchor: \"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models\".\n19\t   - The leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets, and its six component benchmarks are named without citations. HELM and MTEB are cited.\n20\t   - Search topics: Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; IFEval, BBH, GPQA, MuSR and MMLU-Pro papers.\n21\t\n22\t## Missing literatures\n23\t- Cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\n24\t- Adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\n25\t- Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\n26\t- Empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy\n27\t\n28\t## Deliberately not flagged\n29\t- Rank-Gauss transform and kNN: standard components, and the draft already states them as such.\n30\t- Numeric results and protocol facts: grounded in evidence files.\n31\t- Limitations and discussion text: deliberately self-critical, with no external claims that need support.\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"060f18bd-a66c-4a96-b928-c042e7e3464d","uuid":"b8512f48-9627-437f-bca7-19c04f35dafc","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:43:05.068Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well supported overall: the methods (EM, stacking, NNLS, submodular selection, ridge), item-level cheaper-evaluation work, and latent-capability work are all cited, and the quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. Three targeted gaps remain. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **related-work-comparators** (Related Work, high, comparator)\n   - Anchor: \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices\"; \"here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\".\n   - The closest prior work predicts unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, applies low-rank or matrix-completion methods to LLM score matrices, or selects what to run adaptively. The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources and item-level work. The paper's differentiation claim is therefore not positioned against its direct competitors.\n   - Search topics: predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a benchmark subset; matrix completion or low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark matrices; adaptive or IRT-based active selection for LLM evaluation.\n\n2. **intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim** (Introduction, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"are known to overlap in what they measure\".\n   - The premise that motivates budgeted evaluation is cited only to the suites themselves, which do not document redundancy.\n   - Search topics: correlation or redundancy among LLM benchmarks; low-dimensional structure in benchmark scores.\n\n3. **data-open-llm-leaderboard-context** (Method, protocol data paragraph, medium, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models\".\n   - The leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets, and its six component benchmarks are named without citations. HELM and MTEB are cited.\n   - Search topics: Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; IFEval, BBH, GPQA, MuSR and MMLU-Pro papers.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\n- Adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\n- Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\n- Empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Rank-Gauss transform and kNN: standard components, and the draft already states them as such.\n- Numeric results and protocol facts: grounded in evidence files.\n- Limitations and discussion text: deliberately self-critical, with no external claims that need support.\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
