{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KHiiG6T6Ts5XtLjpeCRhmc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"status\": \"ready\",\n3\t  \"summary\": \"The draft is broadly well cited, but the nearest comparator literature (benchmark-level score prediction, matrix completion and adaptive testing on LLM score matrices), the Open LLM Leaderboard data source, and the intro's benchmark-redundancy premise each need 1-3 targeted citations.\",\n4\t  \"missing_literatures\": [\n5\t    \"cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\",\n6\t    \"adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\",\n7\t    \"Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\",\n8\t    \"empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy and correlation\"\n9\t  ],\n10\t  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n11\t    {\n12\t      \"target_id\": \"related-work-comparators\",\n13\t      \"section\": \"related work\",\n14\t      \"priority\": \"high\",\n15\t      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n16\t      \"reason\": \"The closest prior work is benchmark-level: predicting unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, low-rank or matrix-completion imputation of LLM score matrices, and adaptive or active selection of what to run. The Related Work text names matrix completion as 'the standard alternatives for filling score matrices' but cites only generic sources (Candes, Salakhutdinov). The novelty claim ('here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run') is therefore not positioned against the work that most directly competes with it.\",\n17\t      \"anchor_text\": \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices; These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\",\n18\t      \"search_topics\": [\n19\t        \"predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a subset of benchmark scores\",