{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011XS2KymtsDePpxS5t8iMkX","type":"tool_result","content":"36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n109:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n120:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n152:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n162:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n233:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n239:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n290:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n293:Entry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"060f18bd-a66c-4a96-b928-c042e7e3464d","uuid":"3c396550-6dc3-483e-a358-369f05ecab7a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:43:09.433Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"36:Evaluating a new model on every benchmark of a leaderboard is expensive, yet historical score matrices suggest that benchmarks are strongly redundant. We study the cold-start version of this problem: given a model$\\times$benchmark history with missing cells, commit to $k$ benchmarks before seeing the new model, run only those, and impute the rest. We evaluate a selector--imputer pair that combines a rank-Gauss transform, an EM-fitted Gaussian with missing data, cross-validated wrapper selection, and a per-target non-negative stack of imputers. Evaluation uses held-out models from model families disjoint from the history on three public leaderboards (MTEB, Open LLM Leaderboard, HELM Lite). The pair attains pooled normalized RMSE 0.562, a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column-mean imputation, and beats permuted-observation controls by a wide margin, but its skill over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016. On MTEB the error falls from 0.787 at $k{=}2$ to 0.638 at $k{=}20$ with sharply diminishing returns. Selector and imputer are scored jointly, three seeds do not resample held-out models, and no component ablation was run, so we do not attribute the gain to either part.\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. These works reduce item counts; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run.\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the objective is submodular \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular,Krause2007ObservationSelection,Krause2008NearOptimalSensor}. Stacked generalization combines predictors with weights fitted on held-out predictions \\citep{Wolpert1992Stacked,Breiman1996Stacked}, and ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970Ridge} is the linear baseline used by the evaluator.\n107:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n109:\\paragraph{Episodes.} Each seed runs 14 episodes: MTEB at $k\\in\\{2,5,10,20\\}$; Open LLM Leaderboard at $k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$; HELM Lite at $k\\in\\{2,3,5\\}$; plus evaluator-owned controls. In a \\emph{permuted} control, held-out models' observed values on the selected benchmarks are cyclically reassigned to other held-out models (MTEB $k{=}10$, Open LLM $k{=}2$, HELM $k{=}3$); in a \\emph{constant} control the observations are replaced by column means (MTEB $k{=}10$). Controls separate genuine cross-model information from the imputer's marginal prior. The seeds (201, 202, 203) reach only the candidate's random state; the held-out sets, baselines and controls are fixed by hashing.\n120:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n152:The mean reduction per additional benchmark shrinks from about 0.022 between $k{=}2$ and $5$, to 0.012 between 5 and 10, to 0.003 between 10 and 20 (differences of the four tabulated means divided by the number of added benchmarks). This supports the diminishing-returns part of H2 on MTEB, with four budgets and no uncertainty estimate for each point. Even at $k{=}20$, i.e.\\ about 7\\% of the 297 columns, the typical per-cell error is still about 0.64 history standard deviations. We cannot say what $k$ is ``enough'': that depends on an error tolerance we did not define, and the curve has no baseline at each $k$ (the retained metrics report baseline skill only pooled over budgets). The curve is measured for one leaderboard; the Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite grids ($k\\le3$ of 6 and $k\\le5$ of 10) were not retained as per-$k$ curves.\n162:$\\nrmse$ is 0.703 on MTEB, 0.501 on HELM Lite and 0.482 on the Open LLM Leaderboard (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}b), so H4 holds in the sense that error differs. It is not a clean difficulty ranking: the leaderboards differ in the number of benchmarks (297, 10, 6), history size (124, 66, 3371 models), $k$ grids, and what $k$ means as a fraction of the columns. MTEB lists embedding models over many task types, which is also a different object from generative language models; none of the three results transfers to the others. % @evidence[[analysis/protocol_facts.json#final]]\n233:Open LLM Leaderboard & 9c09a7c & 6 & 3371 & 300 & 2314 / 266 & 1, 2, 3\\\\\n239:\\noindent Score definitions: MTEB uses each task's main score averaged over the entries of the first available split (test, validation, dev, train), taken from the model revision directory with the most task files; Open LLM Leaderboard uses raw scores of IFEval, BBH, MATH Level 5, GPQA, MUSR and MMLU-PRO, dropping rows with a missing score; HELM Lite uses the ten scenario columns of the core-scenarios table, excluding the mean-win-rate column. Held-out MTEB and HELM Lite models may lack some scores; those cells are not scored. Per-seed pooled scores of the sealed evaluation were 0.4330 (seed 201), 0.4273 (202) and 0.4328 (203), taking 157.4, 160.6 and 161.0 seconds for 14 episodes each. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.evidence.episodes]]\n290:The following were not run and are not claimed: component ablations (the ablation directory of the run is missing); empirical verification of the trace objective as expected error; bootstrap or model-resampling intervals; per-leaderboard $k$ curves for Open LLM Leaderboard and HELM Lite as retained metrics; baseline $\\nrmse$ values per budget; additional leaderboards such as LiveBench, BigCodeBench or AlpacaEval, which the task description lists as optional.\n293:Entry point: a single Python file exposing a \\texttt{handle} request function (operations \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{load\\_history}, \\texttt{select}, \\texttt{predict}); dependencies are NumPy only; behaviour is deterministic given the seed except for the wall-clock guards. Figures and tables in this paper come from \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py} (Figure~\\ref{fig:main}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_protocol\\_facts.py} (Table~\\ref{tab:data}), \\texttt{scripts/extract\\_search\\_metrics.py} (Tables~\\ref{tab:search} and~\\ref{tab:seeds}); the sealed metrics are read from the evaluation registry. Data sources are public: MTEB results (CC0-1.0), Open LLM Leaderboard contents (no dataset licence declared on its card), and HELM Lite release v1.13.0 (scenario data under per-scenario licences, not individually verified). Literature retrieval used only titles from the starter literature and standard methodology references; no material from the excluded publication was knowingly accessed.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
