he unit of selection is a whole benchmark and the target is every benchmark not run') but cites only item-level work; the closest competitors predict unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones. Targets gap related-work-comparators.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"matrix completion low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark score matrix for LLM evaluation\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Related Work calls matrix completion 'the standard alternatives for filling score matrices' but cites only generic sources (Candes, Salakhutdinov); need work applying it to LLM score matrices. Targets gap related-work-comparators.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"adaptive testing item response theory active selection of evaluations for large language models\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Adaptive or IRT-based selection of what to run is the third competing direction named in the gap memo; existing bibliography has only Embretson (textbook), Vania (test-set comparison), and item-reduction papers. Targets gap related-work-comparators.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"redundancy and correlation among LLM benchmarks low-dimensional structure of benchmark scores\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Introduction says benchmark suites 'are known to overlap in what they measure' but cites only the suites themselves; need empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy. BenchBench and Observational Scaling Laws are already cited, so the query is aimed at redundancy/correlation analyses beyond those. Targets gap intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard v2 evaluation of open language models\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The Open LLM Leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets (3371 history and 300 held-out models) and has no citation, while HELM and MTEB do. Targ